{
  "_comment": "Canonical Sleeper DEFAULT scoring charts (stat-key -> weight), keyed by the ADP/format ids this project uses (std / half_ppr / ppr / 2qb). Flat {stat: weight} dicts in Sleeper's own scoring_settings vocabulary -- same shape as sleepermetrics.scoring.rules_from() -- so a chart can stand in for a league's live scoring_settings where no league is loaded. The four charts differ ONLY by 'rec' (0 / 0.5 / 1 / 1); superflex/2QB is a roster-slot difference, not a scoring one, so 2qb == ppr. Derived by reconciling 39 real public Sleeper leagues (modal weight per key), with pass_int set to Sleeper's true default -1, and cross-checked against ESPN's published standard scoring and the profootballnetwork / fantasypointcalculators / Sleeper-support references. VERIFIED: the std/half_ppr/ppr charts reproduce Sleeper's own pts_std / pts_half_ppr / pts_ppr EXACTLY for every offensive player (1206/1206 player-weeks across 2023-2025) and every kicker (60/60). DST keys are the documented Sleeper defaults but do NOT reproduce Sleeper's DST pts_* exactly -- Sleeper scores team defense from a richer vocabulary (yards-allowed tiers, forced punts, 3-and-outs, return TDs folded into 'td') that a linear chart over the basic keys can't express; this is the same reason sleepermetrics.scoring only scores offensive lineups. Not league-specific, not a parity artifact.",
